  "markdown_report": "The recommended first experiment is **convex recent-context gated SASRec with uniform multi-negative candidate cross-entropy**. It targets stale historical influence and broadly plausible but weak immediate matches, while testing the observed ranking-separation problem. Improvements remain unverified.\n\n### Insights and directions\n\n**Starting-point insights.** (1) SASRec already learns selective historical attention, including recency. Explicit recent pooling therefore needs a controlled comparison. (2) Users report historical interests overwhelming current purpose. Convex fusion can attenuate the historical branch; the previous additive residual cannot explicitly do so. (3) Electronics has 14.95% sampled wrong ranks and a −0.4812 tenth-percentile margin. These motivate stronger discrimination, but do not establish validation HR@10 gains or severe embedding collapse.\n\n**Related-work insights.** (1) MSMT-LSI and Mixture of Sequence motivate temporal fusion, without establishing this variant’s effectiveness. (2) gSASRec uses generalized BCE; eSASRec combines sampled softmax with architectural changes. Neither isolates our proposed loss. (3) Enhanced BPR motivates avoiding aggressive hard mining. Observed-positive exclusion still leaves unobserved relevant negatives.\n\n### Framework\n\n| Direction | Existing mechanism | Proposed minimal test | Remaining gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Temporal relevance | Multiscale encoders | Normalized convex recent-context gate | Recency is not verified intent |\n| Ranking discrimination | Multi-negative losses | Uniform candidate cross-entropy | Restricted sampling and false negatives |\n| Functional relevance | Transition/text branches | Defer initially | Transitions do not verify compatibility |\n\n### Candidate assessment\n\nScores are originality / potential / user alignment / model alignment / implementation difficulty; higher difficulty means harder. Each entry assesses every dimension positively and negatively.\n\n| Idea | Scores | Positive / negative assessments |\n|---|---|---|\n| A: additive recent gate | 3/5/6/4/2 | O: targeted / familiar; P: extensible / retains historical coefficient; U: adds recent evidence / cannot attenuate history; M: context test / uncertain separation; D: small / masking needed |\n| B: multi-negative CE | 2/7/4/8/3 | O: targeted / established; P: reusable / sampling sensitive; U: discrimination / no temporal mechanism; M: targets wrong ranks / transfer uncertain; D: batched / memory cost |\n| C: A+B | 3/6/7/8/4 | O: combined / incremental; P: ablatable / confounded without controls; U: recent evidence / stale branch persists; M: discrimination / false negatives; D: modest / predictor parity |\n| **D: convex gate+B** | **3/7/8/8/5** | O: coherent revision / established components; P: interpretable / limited novelty; U: attenuates stale context and sharpens matches / cannot verify prerequisites; M: targets wrong ranks / gains unverified; D: feasible / sampling and controls require care |\n\n### Selected experiment\n\nAt 0% progress, choose D. Fuse consistently normalized SASRec and causal five-item pooled features using `(1−g)h+gr`; initialize gate weights to zero and bias to −2. Train with 64 uniform negatives and temperature 1. This is candidate-set CE, not corrected full-catalog softmax. Preserve duplicate samples.\n\nCompare baseline, gate-only, loss-only and combined models under identical checkpoint rules; fixed convex and additive controls follow only if budget remains. Track branch norms, gates, per-category margins and GPU seconds. Select by pinned four-category macro validation HR@10. Paired stratified bootstrap intervals are exploratory and do not remove selection optimism. Sampled HR is not full-catalog HR.\n\nRecent fusion addresses stale influence; context-conditioned discrimination targets broadly plausible associations. Neither establishes compatibility or fulfilled demand.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Replace the proposed additive recent residual with learned convex fusion between consistently normalized SASRec features and a causal recent-context branch, and compare single-negative BCE against uniform multi-negative candidate cross-entropy. Preserve independent dataset models and all four routing paths. The combined hypothesis targets stale historical influence and weak immediate relevance, plus measured positive–negative ranking errors; these are intended mechanisms, not demonstrated improvements.",
    "motivation": "Electronics has the weakest measured separation: 14.95% wrong ranks, 21.15% margins below 0.5, and a −0.4812 tenth-percentile margin. Simulator feedback identifies overly broad associations and insufficient emphasis on current interests. Embedding similarity around 0.54–0.56 does not establish severe collapse, so collapse regularization remains unjustified. SASRec already attends selectively to recent history, making an explicit recency branch potentially redundant. Convex fusion fixes the original additive mechanism’s inability to reduce the coefficient on historical features, but cannot guarantee removal of stale information inside either branch. Originality and future-potential ratings are reduced because both mechanisms are established and their combination is incremental; difficulty increases because reproducible sampling, normalization controls, diagnostics and predictor parity require care.",
    "implementation_notes": "Keep DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, unchanged splits, four categories and dataset routing. Write artifacts beneath the trial directory. Respect SASREC_EPOCHS, whose supplied environment default is 200. Retain the baseline optimizer, learning rate, batch size, sequence length, hidden dimension, dropout and checkpoint schedule unless explicitly recorded as a separate intervention; serialize their resolved values and software versions. No outcomes or baseline settings are inferred here.\n\nAt every valid prediction position, pool the last five nonpadding input embeddings at or before that position, divide by the valid count and project to the SASRec hidden dimension. Use unscaled shared item embeddings for pooling. Normalize the historical and projected recent branches with separate non-affine LayerNorm operations of equal epsilon, then fuse with a scalar sigmoid gate per position conditioned on their concatenation. Initialize gate weights to zero and bias to -2, giving initial recency weight approximately 0.119. This is baseline-favoring, not exactly baseline-preserving: normalization and the nonzero recent contribution alter predictions. Include a normalized-backbone control if attributing gains specifically to fusion. Apply no additional normalization after fusion. Zero padding outputs. Implement combined causal and padding masks without fully masked attention rows producing NaNs; any safe padding-query workaround must leave valid-query attention unchanged. PyTorch boolean attention masks use True to block positions.\n\nUse K=64 uniform negative draws with replacement for each valid training position, with temperature tau=1. Sampling support A_u is the category catalog excluding padding and that user's complete observed training history; exclude the current target explicitly as a safeguard. This uses training data only, including later training interactions, never validation or test labels. Retain duplicate negatives and their multiplicity. Skip and count positions with empty support. The training sampler must retain user IDs internally when it constructs sequences; the diagnostic batch contract (None, sequence, positive, negative) does not identify users and must not be used to guess them from truncated histories. Preserve that public loader contract through a diagnostic adapter.\n\nThe chosen loss is ordinary cross-entropy over one deterministic positive and 64 sampled negatives. Call it candidate-set CE; it is neither gBCE nor an importance-corrected full-catalog softmax approximation. No logQ correction is applied. A separate restricted-partition experiment would add log(|A_u|/K) to negative logits after division by temperature, preserving duplicates. That estimates a restricted partition inside the logarithm, not an unbiased full-catalog loss, and cannot restore historical items with zero sampling probability. Do not silently mix this objective with the selected one.\n\nBound memory by chunking valid positions and negative scoring. Weight each chunk by its valid-position count divided by the batch total, accumulate gradients, and take one optimizer step per original batch. Recompute chunk features or retain the shared computation graph correctly; detaching features would stop backbone learning. Use a dedicated seeded sampler so diagnostic calls cannot perturb training randomness.\n\nPredeclare four primary configurations: unchanged baseline, gate-only with baseline loss, loss-only with unchanged backbone, and combined. These isolate each intervention relative to baseline; the combined model tests interaction. Normalized-backbone, fixed convex gate at sigmoid(-2), and matched additive fusion are secondary controls only if resources remain. Match normalization, window and initial gate weight across fusion controls. Preserve baseline checkpoint evaluation frequency and selection rule, and choose the final configuration exclusively by pinned macro validation HR@10. Resolve exact ties by a predeclared simpler-model preference. Use the same 200 users per category and pinned 99 unique negatives throughout. One extra hit changes category HR by 0.005 and macro HR by 0.00125.\n\nThe 43,200 GPU-second budget includes all training, diagnostics and failed attempts; concurrent slots do not multiply it. Reserve 4,320 seconds for diagnostics and failures, and cap each primary configuration at 9,720 seconds across all four category models. Execute sequentially on one GPU and save resumable, timed checkpoints. Equal wall-time caps do not ensure equal optimizer steps; report actual epochs, steps and truncation. Do not claim matched training exposure if a cap truncates a run. Secondary controls require unused resources within the same budget, not additional allocation.\n\nIf the host exposes per-user validation hits, save paired binary outcomes. Bootstrap 200 users with replacement independently within each category, using identical resampled indices for each compared model, then average category differences equally. Use 10,000 replicates with a separate analysis seed of 42 and report exploratory 95% percentile intervals. These condition on candidates and training seed and do not correct repeated checkpoint/configuration selection. Do not modify the evaluator to obtain unavailable outputs. Sampled HR@10 must not be interpreted as full-catalog HR@10.\n\nUpdate the predictor writer to serialize the chosen architecture, fusion mode, window, normalization epsilon, gate parameters and all weights, and instantiate the identical model in working/predict.py. Preserve category routing and the host prediction interface. Before final evaluation, check that evaluation-mode training and generated-predictor scores agree on public training-derived sequences and candidate IDs.\n\nPreserve DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). view.model remains the actual trained nn.Module; log2feats returns the fused features and predict_score uses the trained shared output embeddings. Return metrics and metric_definitions with actual measured margins, gate statistics and branch norms. Label added metrics clearly and retain existing metrics. Category breakdowns are unavailable when categories are absent; never fabricate them. Keep item_emb, user_train, meta_data and review_data compatibility attributes, using empty unavailable metadata rather than invented fields.\n\nOnly public histories, timestamps, titles and descriptions are permitted; this experiment needs histories alone. Review ratings/text, item ratings/categories/prices/stores and simulator diversity/conformity traits are unavailable despite the generic example schema. Twenty real training users, five per category, receive full-catalog unseen-item recommendations through the native simulator. Their judgments remain qualitative evidence, not intent labels or the selection metric. Recent preferences may reflect already-satisfied demand, and a recency shortcut can amplify that error. Track repeated-recent-item dominance, gate saturation and sparse-history behavior without interpreting them as verified compatibility. Avoid user-ID features, validation-trained gates, pseudo-labels derived from simulator judgments, and aggressive hard mining. Report that exclusions cannot eliminate unobserved positives and that validation selection remains susceptible to overfitting.",
    "pseudocode": "class RecentConvexSASRec(SASRec):\n    def log2feats(self, sequence):\n        valid = sequence.ne(0)\n        h = super().log2feats(sequence)  # safe causal/padding attention\n        r = causal_masked_mean(self.item_emb(sequence), window=5)\n        h = self.history_norm(h)        # non-affine LayerNorm\n        r = self.recent_norm(self.recent_projection(r))\n        g = sigmoid(self.gate(concat(h, r)))  # scalar; W=0, bias=-2\n        return where(valid[..., None], (1-g)*h + g*r, 0)\n\nfor configuration in PREDECLARED_PRIMARY_CONFIGURATIONS:\n    reset_training_seed(42)\n    for category in FOUR_DATASETS:\n        model = instantiate_configuration(configuration, category)\n        # Sampler owns user IDs; diagnostic adapter retains the required tuple.\n        for users, sequence, positive in internal_training_sampler:\n            optimizer.zero_grad()\n            valid_positions = positive.ne(0)\n            n_valid = count(valid_positions)\n            if n_valid == 0:\n                continue\n            features = model.log2feats(sequence)\n            chunks = chunk_positions(valid_positions)\n            for chunk_index, positions in enumerate(chunks):\n                negatives = uniform_with_replacement(\n                    support=catalog_without_padding_or_complete_training_history,\n                    users=users[positions.batch_index],\n                    exclude_targets=positive[positions], K=64,\n                    generator=dedicated_training_generator)\n                # Handle empty supports before calculating the batch denominator.\n                candidates = concat(positive[positions, None], negatives)\n                logits = model.predict_score(features[positions], candidates) / 1.0\n                loss = cross_entropy(logits, zeros(len(positions), dtype=long))\n                weighted_loss = loss * len(positions) / n_valid\n                weighted_loss.backward(retain_graph=chunk_index < len(chunks)-1)\n            optimizer.step()\n            enforce_shared_gpu_time_budget()\n        save_checkpoint_and_identical_predictor_writer()\n        run_required_diagnostics_without_changing_training_rng()\n\n# Gate-only and baseline use their original single-negative loss instead.\n# Host performs pinned validation; use its macro HR@10 for selection.\n# Save actual compute exposure and paired hits if the host makes them available.\n# No validation outcomes or held-out results are asserted.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "The revision connects a specific temporal failure with a separately testable discrimination intervention and corrects the additive fusion mechanism.",
      "negative": "Convex gates, recent pooling and multi-negative candidate cross-entropy are established techniques; this is an incremental diagnostic adaptation."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 7,
      "positive": "Controlled results can establish whether temporal representation or objective quality warrants further work before adding text, transitions or intent experts.",
      "negative": "The method cannot establish compatibility or fulfilled demand, and fixed-seed sampled validation may favor effects that do not generalize."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "The model change remains small and preserves shared item scoring and the SASRec compatibility interfaces.",
      "negative": "Correct user-aware exclusions, causal pooling, padding safety, chunked gradients, resource accounting and generated-predictor parity require substantial care."
    }
  },
